.TH CTPOOL_WAIT 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ctpool_wait \- block until the pool is idle
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

void ctpool_wait(ctpool pool);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Blocks until the task queue is empty and every active task has
stopped. It doesn't shut the pool down: every worker thread stays
alive, and the caller can submit new tasks after the call returns.

Contract: no other thread should submit a task while ctpool_wait runs,
because with an unbounded queue a submission from another thread can
keep this call blocked for ever.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
pool   Thread pool handle (CTPOOL_INVALID or a stale/already-destroyed handle is a no-op)
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
ctpool_wait waits for the queue and for the tasks that workers run,
but not for the on_complete callbacks of discarded tasks. A
ctpool_shutdown_immediate(3) on another thread empties the queue at
once and then calls the on_complete of each discarded task, with
ran == false, on its own thread, so ctpool_wait can return while those
callbacks are still running. If you need to know that all of them
have finished, wait for the ctpool_shutdown_immediate call itself to
return.

By default a pool that a child inherited across fork(2) is treated as
idle, because fork(2) doesn't copy the pool's worker threads into the
child. ctpool_wait on such a pool in the child returns at once instead
of waiting for progress that no worker thread in that process can ever
make.

A call to ctpool_wait on pool from inside a task running on one of
pool's worker threads, or from the on_complete callback of that task,
is also treated as idle by default and returns at once. The pool
counts the calling task among its active tasks until that task
returns, so without this rule a wait here would deadlock the calling
worker against itself.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_shutdown_drain (3),
.BR ctpool_shutdown_immediate (3)
